            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Adds values before and after the existing ones along a dim: zeros (or any constant), copies of the end values, a mirror image, or a wrap-around from the other end.\n\nUse it before filtering or windowing so the result keeps the input's length and the ends are not lost, to bring sequences of different lengths to the same length so they can be stacked, or to give an image a border.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.pad <before> <after> <mode>\nDefaults: 1 and 1, constant. One number pads both ends by it. The mode can also be\ngiven here: constant, replicate, reflect, circular (or numpy's names edge and wrap).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\nbefore, after (int boxes, also inlets):\nHow many values to add at the start and at the end.\n\nvalue (float box, also an inlet):\nWhat constant padding adds.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nThe dim padded along; -1, the last, by default.\n\nmode:\nAn option, under the node. constant adds the value; replicate repeats the end values;\nreflect mirrors the values next to the ends (1 2 3 -> 3 2 1 2 3); circular continues from the other end, as if the values went round in a loop.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npadded:\nThe longer tensor.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 2 3 4 padded by 2 at each end: with zeros (top), and reflected (bottom), which continues the shape smoothly and is the usual choice before a filter.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.pad is the numpy version. t.cat joins tensors end to end. t.unfold and t.convolve are often padded first.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1150,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Adds values before and after the existing ones along a dim: zeros (or any constant), copies of the end values, a mirror image, or a wrap-around from the other end.\n\nUse it before filtering or windowing so the result keeps the input's length and the ends are not lost, to bring sequences of different lengths to the same length so they can be stacked, or to give an image a border.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.pad <before> <after> <mode>\nDefaults: 1 and 1, constant. One number pads both ends by it. The mode can also be\ngiven here: constant, replicate, reflect, circular (or numpy's names edge and wrap).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\nbefore, after (int boxes, also inlets):\nHow many values to add at the start and at the end.\n\nvalue (float box, also an inlet):\nWhat constant padding adds.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nThe dim padded along; -1, the last, by default.\n\nmode:\nAn option, under the node. constant adds the value; replicate repeats the end values;\nreflect mirrors the values next to the ends (1 2 3 -> 3 2 1 2 3); circular continues from the other end, as if the values went round in a loop.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npadded:\nThe longer tensor.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n1 2 3 4 padded by 2 at each end: with zeros (top), and reflected (bottom), which continues the shape smoothly and is the usual choice before a filter.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.pad is the numpy version. t.cat joins tensors end to end. t.unfold and t.convolve are often padded first."
        },
